      <section  data-background-image="images/puzzle.jpg">
        <h1>Design goal: Arbitrary data structures</h1>
        
<div class="columns">
  <div class="column" style="flex: 0 0 95%; max-width: 95vw;">
    <p><hl>cooker</hl> uses root trees and root serialization for data exchange. </p>
    <p>
    <ul class="morespace">
        <li>Plugins select which branches to read &mdash; either from input file or output file (= from other plugin run at the same time)</li>
        <li>Plugins can create new branches in the output file.</li>
        <li>Full control over data structure. </li>
        <li>Tree data structure also documents data interface between plugins.</li>
    </ul></p>
  </div>
  <img class="maxcolumn" style="flex: 0 0 15%; max-width: 25vw;" src="images/BHtree.png" />
</div>
                </section>
            
     
            
            <section  data-background-image="images/puzzle.jpg">
        <h1>Less warts, less boiler-plate code, less things to get wrong</h1>
           <p style="margin-top:100px"><hl>cooker</hl> uses recipes to select plugins / functions in plugin.</p>
                
           <p> Plugin can implement functions for three phases:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Startup: Before event processing</li>
                <li>Process: Called for each event  (multiple loops over file possible)</li>
                <li>Finalize: After event processing</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="fragment" style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 30px;">
              <div>
                <p>For free:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li> Event loop, file handling</li>
                    <li> Automatic handling of event weights <br>(when using overloaded histogram functions)</li>
                    <li> Automatic support for multiple input files</li>
                    <li> Automatic support for MPI-style multi-processing</li>
                    <li> Automatic support for event-navigation in GUI</li>
                    <li> Robust init file handling</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
              <img src="images/visco.png" style="height: 600px; width: auto; margin-top: 20px;" />
            </div>
            </section>
            
            <section  data-background-image="images/puzzle.jpg">
        <h1>Blinding in MUSE: The threat model</h1>
               <h2> Situation:</h2> MUSE is trying to measure the proton radius with high precision. This is a highly debated field.   
               <div class="fragment">

               <h2 style="margin-top:50px;"> Want to protect against:</h2>
               <ul>
                <li> Confirmation bias: Researchers have expectations for the radius which might unconsciously bias analysis.</li>
                <li> Premature declare-victory trap: A result that looks "right" might short-cut further systematics checks. </li>
                <li> Unintended publication: Data shown at conferences might be close enough to final results that they could be (mis)interpreted by outside parties.</li>
                <li> Accidental unblinding.</li>
               </ul>
            </div>
               <div class="fragment">
               <h2 style="margin-top:50px;"> What is not included in our threat model</h2>
               <ul>
                <li> Malicious actors: Open data and open code.</li>
               </ul>
      </div>
</section>

    <section  data-background-image="images/puzzle.jpg">
        <h1>Approach in MUSE</h1>
            <p> MUSE is a high precision <hl>cross section</hl> measurement and has several types of results:</p>
            <ul>
            
                <li> Cross sections: $\left.\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}\right|_{\text{data}}   / \left.\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}\right|_{\text{MC}}$</li>
                <li> Lepton ratios: Ratio of cross section for different leptons</li>

                <li> Charge asymmetries: Ratio of cross section for $l^+$ vs $l^-$</li>
                <li> Radii: $\approx$ Slopes of cross sections</li>
            </ul>
     <p>$\Longrightarrow$ Need to affect shape, not only normalization. Need to affect data and MC differently. Need to affect different leptons/charges differently. </p>
<div class="fragment">
<ul class="morespace">

    <li> We blind on the track level: tracking is slow and will only be done centrally.</li>
    <li> Cooker framework recognizes track-plugin output and blinds it</li>
    <li> Tracks are selected with an angle dependent probability</li>
    <li>Selected tracks are marked and encrypted: Normal analysis doesn't see them</li>
    <li> For unblinding: Decrypt, run all "downstream" plugins (fast)</li>
</ul>

</div>
</section>


    <section  data-background-image="images/puzzle.jpg">
        <h1>Blinding probability</h1>
   $$ P(\theta)= 0.2(A+0.3\cos(B\theta)) \frac{3-\theta}{3}$$
         <!-- ...existing code... -->
<ul>
    <li> Suppresses more at forward angles where we have best statistics</li>
    <li> Parameters A and B: randomly generated from a fixed seed.</li>
    <li> Different for species, beam momentum, beam charge, data vs. MC.</li>
</ul>
<div class="fragment" style="margin-top:70px;">
 Blinding will always reduce rate at small angles $\rightarrow$ <hl>Can guess direction?</hL>
</div>
<div class="fragment">
    <hl> No!</hl> Results are always ratios. Final sign of correction is unknown!
    <br/>
<img style="height: 600px; margin-top: 50px" src="images/blindexample.png" />
</div>



</section>

    <section  data-background-image="images/puzzle.jpg">
        <h1>Unblinding strategy</h1>
        <h2>Key management</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
        Decryption keys for tracks are stored in the output files, encrypted via Public-Key-Cryptography. </li>
        <li> Three people have keys. Need keys from two people to decrypt. No single point of failure, no unilateral unblinding.</li>
       <li> Can unblind selectively by species, momentum, time chunk</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="fragment">
    <h2 style="margin-top: 50px;"">
Planned schedule
    </h2>
    <ol>
        <li> Unblind pion data first: Less controversal, smaller bias. </li>
     <li> Unblind TPE data (MC cancels)</li>
     <li> Unblind all</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
    In each group, we will first unblind step by step a fraction of the data, either by momentum or by time chunk. This allows us to check for unexpected issues before unblinding the full data set.</p>
    </div>

<div class="fragment">For more details on our blinding scheme, see arxiv:2310.11469  <img style="height: 200px; margin-top: 50px" src="images/blindingQR.png" /></div>
  
</section>


